			)
		)
		(uuid "ff2acece-df86-43ae-a56d-8fce0f6bed95")
	)
	(text_box "Calculated using this site: https://www.random-science-tools.com/electronics/schmitt-trigger-calculator.htm"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 96.52 237.49 0)
		(size 85.09 6.35)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type solid)
		)
		(fill
			(type none)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "8faecf2e-ec23-4c8b-8a9d-6955d0e52a0b")
	)
	(text_box "Happily the RVQ040N has a fairly beefy body diode (forward voltage 1.2V, max pulsed current 10+ A). This provides some inbuilt ESD and flyback current protection on DIO, so if it goes above GND or below V_{LOGIC}, the voltage will be limited by the diode."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 215.9 100.33 0)
		(size 46.99 13.97)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type solid)
		)
		(fill
			(type none)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "9dafd676-f249-48cf-9a5a-858e5b95ab96")
	)
	(text_box "RVQ040N has a drain-source resistance of 45 mΩ and a max current of 4 A. This means one channel can supply a great deal of current, and the current capacity of the DIO channels is limited by power supply constraints.\n- Per-channel limit: 4A source/sink\n- When running off internal power: Limited by internal rail capacity\n- When running off external power: Individual channels can reach 4A. Total power must not exceed connector and PCB trace limits."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 289.56 54.61 0)
		(size 54.61 24.13)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type solid)
		)
		(fill
			(type none)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "a1a4fb7b-cb56-4565-8322-c61d665d074d")
	)
	(text_box "Schmitt trigger implemented using an LM311 open-collector comparator.\nThis circuit provides hysterisis on the digital input, transitioning from low to high at 3.5V and from high to low at 1.67V (the ideal transition point would be 1.5V, but the 10k pullup on COMP_OUT skews this a bit).\n"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 102.87 219.71 0)
		(size 60.96 15.24)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type solid)
		)
		(fill
			(type none)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "c0bc0f29-1e1a-4e49-9956-7ec69a6f78cc")
	)
	(text_box "To control the high and low output transistors, I selected an RT9624 gate driver chip. This chip uses an internal charge pump to generate a voltage of (VCC + PHASE) to control the upper transistor gate.\nThis chip has some neat features, including automatic shoot-through protection, so when switching, one transistor does not turn on until the gate of the other transistor has gone low.\nIt is controlled via a single tri-state line: pulling PWM high or low turns the upper or lower transistor on, while floating PWM keeps both transistors off. This avoids the need to use a second enable line!\nHowever, there are still a few things I might need to test out more:\n- The datasheet says it has resistors to pull the PWM line to a center voltage when not driven, but doesn't say what values these resistors are. So, I will also need to check the prop time of going from high and low to tri-state.\n- The datasheet example circuit puts a resistor in line with the VCC and BOOT pins, but doesn't say why. I copied this becase I assume there is some reason for it...\n- There are no specs for the input impedance on the PHASE pin. Unclear if this pin draws significant power when both transistors are off. If it does, it could violate the input impedance requirement."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 90.17 53.34 0)
		(size 81.28 48.26)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type solid)
		)
		(fill
			(type none)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "ce22a563-6132-46e8-8f49-4df421c489a0")
	)
	(text_box "For the input stage, we need to reduce the V_{logic}-level signal (24-3.3V) down into something we can use. However, we can't attach a resistor divider directly to the signal, as that would affect the input impedance significantly. So, we buffer it with one channel of an opamp, divide it down by 5x, then output it to the comparator with the other channel.\nThe LM358 opamp chosen actually has such a low bandwidth (like 30kHz) that it also provides some nice low-pass filtering on the logic signal."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 233.68 229.87 0)
		(size 69.85 22.86)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type solid)
		)
		(fill
			(type none)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "cf18aac1-a432-4fff-889a-fc2d89b1a4c5")
	)
	(text_box "The RVQ040N has a gate resistance of 7 ohms, so no gate resistor is needed with the RT9624. This should give a rise and fall time in the 10-20 ns range (between when the gate driver begins pumping current into the gate and when the MOSFET is fully on)."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 214.63 50.8 0)
		(size 44.45 15.24)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type solid)
		)
		(fill
			(type none)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "d2875285-7bb7-4b98-9351-c2e9fb7f3145")
	)
	(text_box "AA3528SURSK LED has forward voltage 1.95V @ 20mA.\nResistor should dissipate around 60mW."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 40.64 189.23 0)
		(size 26.67 12.7)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type solid)
		)
		(fill
			(type none)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "de6b7bde-7852-4ce7-9216-3376f8778a40")
	)
